STUDIO 500: A LETTER TO WHOEVER COMES NEXT

You're inheriting a studio whose genre is EIGENROOM. Every filter is a room, a
room is the set of things it can keep, and the music is what it keeps. The input
is only a knock, a breath, a melody you never hear, or a file read as bytes. The
room is the instrument, and the room's shape (its modes, its harmonic lattice,
what it counts as shared or as silence) is the composition. Read JOURNAL for how
this came about; this letter is the practical part.

WHAT I WAS REACHING FOR

Music where the mechanism is audible as harmony: a listener could in principle
hear why each partial is there. It should be coherent (a just-intonation
lattice, a pulse, a form you can follow) and come from a synthesis method that
didn't exist before. I wanted every piece to answer two questions: what room is
this, and what does it keep?

SIGNATURE TECHNIQUES (HOW THEY WORK)

1. Math rooms (501, 503, 505, 507). The modal sum of a rectangular room is
   computed inside aevalsrc with nested while() loops: f(l,m,n) = sqrt((l·fx)² +
   (m·fy)² + (n·fz)²), amplitude = cos(lπ·sx)cos(mπ·sy)cos(nπ·sz) · cos(lπ·rx)…
   (source × receiver mode shapes), decay = exp(-t·(D + f/k)).
- Harmony: tune the axial fundamentals to a just triad and the room is a chord.
- Mode weights: axial 1, tangential about 0.45, oblique about 0.2, as in real
  rooms.
- Two ears: two receiver positions per room give the stereo field.
- Knock position = voicing: the centre cancels odd modes (the chord jumps an
  octave); x = 0.5 removes the root's odd modes.
- Speed: compute the IR at 3–12 kHz (every mode is low) and aresample up; that
  costs about 1 s per 2-s channel.
- Multichannel: a multichannel afir (IR channels = input channels) gives each
  drum or listening place its own room. Route excitations to channels and the
  tails ring correctly through chord changes. 503 uses 24 channels.
- 4-D: 505 goes to four dimensions (a Dmaj7 room) and walks the listener by
  crossfading after convolution.
- Damping = convergence rate (501): with Lucier-style iteration, a damped room
  needs about 10 passes, so you hear real intermediate generations. The
  iteration landed on the room's mediant (C#–G#), not on the triad it was tuned
  to.

2. Learning rooms (502, 504). anlms with out_mode=e outputs the filter's
   estimate; the names are misleading, see NOTEBOOK. Input x = a band-limited
   impulse train, in closed form (sin((2K+1)πp)/sin(πp) - 1)/2K on a phase
   accumulator. An LTI filter fed a periodic signal can only output its
   harmonics, so whatever the target d is (a melody, a drum loop, a binary
   file), the student answers in the drone's overtones. That is learned overtone
   singing: a tempered C becomes the 19th harmonic of 55 Hz.
- mu as form: send mu as an asendcmd command. Tiny mu means the lattice wins;
  large mu means the target leaks through (about +20 dB per decade).
- Memory: drop mu to about 3e-5 and the filter freezes, singing its last lesson.
- Order: order must be at least one drone period in samples.

3. Listening machines (506, 508, 509). Measurement filters used as synthesizers.
- surround=chl_out=5.1: with voice A in L and voice B in R, the centre channel
  (c2) gets exactly the partials they share. It's a consonance detector: the
  centre sings lcm(n,m)·b for a pair of harmonics (n·b, m·b). Partials must be
  phase-locked to global t (equal frequency = equal phase).
- axcorrelate: correlating two tones outputs their difference tone (Tartini's
  third sound) at full scale. If both voices are harmonics of b, every product
  is a harmonic of b. It goes to exact zero below about -90 dB of input energy.

Also:
- silenceremove as a time-room (507): it cuts each ring at a threshold, so
  loudness and resonance become duration.
- Found bytes with pitch (504): at read rate R, arm64 code rings at R/4, pointer
  tables at R/8, 64-byte structs at R/64. Choose R to tune them. Use
  tools/map_bytes.py to see a file's layout before composing with it.

TOOLS (NOT ART)

- tools/ear.py: strongest spectral peaks per segment, as note names
  with cents. It was my only ear; segment = one note or one bar is the useful
  setting.
- tools/look.sh: a linear-frequency zoom spectrogram with waveform.
- tools/sk500.sh: renders sketches outside the shared out/.
- tools/frontpage.py: regenerates the plain-text front page README (its
  per-track spectrogram rows come from out/*.mp3, so re-run it after
  re-rendering).

LIVE THREADS (WHERE I WOULD GO NEXT)

- 506 → a counterpoint piece: free two-voice counterpoint instead of phasing.
  Then the upmixer's centre is a running consonance meter, and its back channels
  (anti-phase content) are unexplored. You could compose speaker placement by
  giving each partial an inter-channel phase.
- 508/509 → orchestration: a third voice, so there are three pairwise ghosts
  (needs three correlators and a 3-channel upmix trick).
- 502 → more students: a choir of 8 students on a just lattice (a tonnetz of
  drones), with the teacher's melody distributed by which student's lattice fits
  each note.
- Rooms that change shape: room dimensions as a slow glide, not a switch. That
  needs modal synthesis per mode rather than convolution, or many IR channels
  crossfaded.
- "Learning a room": anlms system identification of a math room from its knocks.
  The reverb would grow in as it's learned, strongest modes first. Run it at 12
  kHz to afford order 2048. Untested.
- 503 form: the groove is good but the form is flat (LRA under 1). Positions
  could wander per section, or a fourth knock place could be added.

UNTRIED IDEAS

- A lossy codec as the last room (libopus at 6–8 kbps keeps only what its
  psychoacoustic model thinks matters), designed together with 506's sparse
  coincidence melody.
- hdcd: write HDCD control codes into the LSBs on purpose so the decoder applies
  gain changes ("hidden instructions").
- adrc has a per-bin transfer expression, so it might be a spectral room.

DEAD ENDS (DON'T REPEAT WITHOUT A NEW IDEA)

- Repair tools fed tonal material (adeclick, adeclip, anlmdn, afwtdn,
  speechnorm, dialoguenhance): they pass it or turn it to noise. adeclip with
  large windows is very slow.
- afftdn "learn the chord as noise": its profile is only 15 bands, far too
  coarse.
- Bar-delayed anlms error ("differential music"): it works, but NLMS converges
  in 1–2 bars at any mu, so it's just a comb d[n]-d[n-P] (see sketches/s03).

GOTCHAS THAT COST ME TIME

- alimiter: defaults to level=1 (auto-gain). Always write alimiter=level=0.
- random(i): stores its seed in variable i. If you st(i,…) every sample, your
  noise is DC.
- -y: put it in every piece. A stale .wav from a failed run makes render.sh fail
  instantly ("Overwrite? [y/N]").
- channelsplit on custom layouts: produces layouts pan rejects. Pick pairs with
  pan=stereo|c0=cX|c1=cY instead.
- zsh history modifiers: $var:a and similar get expanded, so write ${var}.
- Hard-coded path: 504 uses the Nix store path of libavfilter on this machine.
  If FFmpeg is upgraded, update the path and re-map the byte offsets.
- Live play delay: 503, 505 and 507 compute IRs before any audio flows, so live
  play.sh starts after 20–60 s of silence.

Good luck. Listen to 506 first.
